{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nDuring a local `make verify` (via `.rebar/scratch/verify-slots/verify-slot.sh`, up to 3 concurrent runs on the host) at commit `e2795a076d1` (worktree `bluewhale-llm-proxy`, 2026-11-22 13:38-15:11 PST), 2 unit tests in unrelated modules hit the 300 s pytest-timeout and crashed their xdist workers. Both were blocked in the same place: a `git` subprocess started by `rebar init`'s config ensures never returned.\n\n- `tests/unit/test_attestation_signing.py::test_sign_manifest_without_kind_omits_field` (worker gw1): `store` fixture (line 41) -> `init_repo` -> `init_core` -> `_run_ensures_logged` -> `run_ensures` -> `_init_ensures._gc_config_unit` (line 70) -> `_git` -> `run_git_write` -> `_with_index_lock_retry` -> `_with_transient_fault_retry` -> `run_git_bounded` -> `run_git` -> `_run_in_group` (gitutil.py:309) -> `subprocess.communicate` waiting in `selectors.select`.\n- `tests/unit/test_compact_sweep_perf.py::test_ledger_is_identical_with_and_without_a_prebuilt_map` (worker gw2): same chain, but from `_init_ensures._merge_ours_unit` (line 84).\n\nThe same suite passed on the parent commit's tree an hour earlier (21766 passed, 51 min). This run took 1 h 30 min (21771 passed, 2 failed), so the host was heavily loaded. Re-running both tests alone (3 times, `-n0`) and both modules under `-n4` passes every time (2/2, 45/45).\n\nA `git config` write should take milliseconds. `run_git_write` is bounded by `_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT` (120 s per the store's own comments), yet these calls were still inside `communicate()` when the 300 s test timeout fired. Either the bound did not apply here, or the retry wrappers re-ran a bounded call enough times to pass 300 s.\n\n## Findings\n- Worst-case wall time of one `run_git_write` call. A timeout returns code 124, which is neither a lock nor a transient-filesystem signature, so neither retry re-runs it; a held `config.lock` fails at once (measured: exit 255 in 1.2 s, no retry). One stalled call therefore costs: up to 30 s waiting for the per-store git-op lock (`_STORE_GIT_LOCK_WAIT_S`, only when a peer holds it) + the 120 s bound (`_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT`) + up to 10 s of process-group reaping (5 s grace + 5 s drain) = 160 s. Theoretical ceiling if every attempt printed a lock or transient signature just before the bound: about 3,970 s (10 x 3 x 130 s + 30 s + backoff); no observed failure takes that path.\n- The bound held in the failing run: both stacks are inside the bounded `communicate()` (`gitutil.py:309`), not reaping or a retry; the 300 s accumulated across sequential bounded calls.\n- Reproduced deterministically with a `git` shim that stalls every `git config` in the tracker before git runs (a stalled filesystem):\n  - Real 120 s bound, the 2 failing tests under `-n2`: both hit the 300 s pytest-timeout with the stack in `_run_in_group` `communicate()` at `gitutil.py:309`, the failing run's signature. The stalled calls were init's own `_ensure_branch_user_config` (`init._git`), before the ensure sweep.\n  - Bound patched to 2 s: `init_repo` issued 7 stalled config calls in the sweep (14.6 s) and reported `gc-config` and `merge-ours` as `changed` although no write happened; both were recorded in `.ensure-applied`, so no later sweep retries them.\n- Defect: init and the ensure units read a timed-out git (124, empty stdout) as an answer (\"unset\", \"absent\") and go on to issue the next git call, which also stalls. Every sequential call pays its own bound, and the result is misreported as converged.\n- The failing run's two stacks (one in `_gc_config_unit` line 70, one in `_merge_ours_unit` line 84) show some calls stalled and others completed, so the host stall was partial, not a constant 120 s per call. What stalled git on the host is not established (no sleep or wake in `pmset -g log` for the window; `git config` alone takes 3 ms with no child processes).\n\n## Approach\nTreat a watchdog-timed-out git in the ensure units as a stop signal, not as an answer. Init's own bootstrap git calls (`init._git`) are out of scope: they also serve auto-init predicates and rebase recovery, and stopping init mid-bootstrap can leave a half-mounted tracker. That is filed as follow-up bug corrupting-plaster-seagull.\n1. `rebar._store.ensures` gains `GitTimedOut(RuntimeError)` and `stop_on_git_timeout(result)`, which raises `GitTimedOut` when `git_outcome.is_watchdog_timeout(result)` and otherwise returns the result.\n2. `_init_ensures._git` returns `stop_on_git_timeout(run_git_write(...))`. Its units (gc-config, merge-ours, gitattributes, gitignore, store-compat) stop at their first timed-out call instead of reading empty output as \"unset\" or \"absent\".\n3. `run_ensures` catches `GitTimedOut`, records that unit `failed` with the timed-out command and the count of units not run in its detail, and runs no further unit. Units not run produce no outcome, so `.ensure-applied` omits them and the next sweep retries them. Other exceptions keep record-and-continue. Units with their own seams (env-id, projects-*, untrack-runtime-markers, snapshot-close-metadata) are unchanged.\n4. `_gc_config_unit` and `_merge_ours_unit` return a `failed` outcome with git's stderr when a write fails (for example a held `config.lock`), instead of `changed`.\n5. The bootstrap pin in `_mount_or_create_branch` (the only non-sweep caller of a unit) calls a new helper `_pin_auto_maintenance(repo)`, which runs `_gc_config_unit(repo)` and on `GitTimedOut` logs a warning and returns, so init proceeds exactly as today, after 1 bound instead of up to 5. `_mount_or_create_branch`'s complexity stays at 18.\n6. Docs: `docs/migrations.md` (the `failed = raised` comment and both statements that the sweep always continues), the `EnsureOutcome`, module and `run_ensures` docstrings in `ensures.py`, and `init._run_ensures_logged`'s docstring.\nEffect: a stalled `git config` costs the ensure sweep one bound (up to 160 s) instead of one per call, and nothing timed out or failed is reported converged. No config key, env var or flag is added; the 120 s bound is unchanged.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_store/ensures.py`: `GitTimedOut`, `stop_on_git_timeout`, the sweep stop, docstrings.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/_init_ensures.py`: `_git` stops on a timeout; write results checked.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/init.py`: `_pin_auto_maintenance`; `_run_ensures_logged` docstring.\n- `docs/migrations.md`: the stop-on-timeout rule and `failed` meaning.\n- `tests/unit/test_ensure_git_timeout.py` (new): regression tests.\n\n## Testing\nIn the new module, the `_init_ensures` git seam stubbed to return a code-124 watchdog result (no real sleeps):\n- Fails on the current tree: with every `config` call timed out, `_gc_config_unit` raises `GitTimedOut` after exactly 1 git call (today: 5 calls and `changed`).\n- `run_ensures` on an initialised store with the stub: `gc-config` is `failed` with the command in its detail, no later unit is invoked (recording stub), `.ensure-applied` omits gc-config and every later unit.\n- Recovery: stub removed, a second `run_ensures` has no `failed` outcome and `.ensure-applied` lists every registry id.\n- Real git with a held `config.lock`: `_gc_config_unit` and `_merge_ours_unit` each return `failed` with \"could not lock config file\", not `changed`.\n- `_pin_auto_maintenance` with the stub returns after 1 git call and logs a warning.\n- A unit raising `ValueError` is still recorded `failed` and the sweep continues.\n- Existing ensure, init and fsck tests pass; `make verify` before push.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The worst-case wall time of one `run_git_write` call, retries included, is measured and stated.\n- [ ] The blocked-`git config` condition is reproduced, or a stated experiment shows why it cannot be, with the evidence recorded on this ticket.\n- [ ] A fix (or a justified close as environmental, with evidence) lands, with a regression test if a code defect is found."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795404847606822001,"uuid":"29fb0b23-dda1-4d17-91c9-819348d37052"}